  "query_preview": "...A weak upper low centered over Mexico will lift to \nthe north through New Mexico and Texas on Friday. The return flow \naround the low continues to bring some moisture into eastern Arizona \nthis afternoon. Some scattered showers with isolated thunderstorms \nwill remain possible into the early evening, mainly towards the AZ/NM \nborder. While we are not expecting any severe storms, there may be \nsome stronger storms with lightning and gusty outflows. \n\nThe rest of the region will continue to remain warm and dry with a \nfew cloud developing as the mid level moisture remains elevated. \nTemperatures will be around 5-10 degrees above normal on Friday. \n\nFor Friday.... the low lifts northward into NM/TX, ending the return \nflow and shifting the moisture off to our east, bringing an end to \nany shower activity across our forecast area. There remains an \noutside chance of a shower along the AZ/NM border. Overall, expect \nFriday to be warm and dry with westerly afternoon breezes.\n\nThe models remain keyed in on a deepening west coast trough, \nstarting this weekend and continuing through much of next week. The \nposition of the trough will determine what kind of weather we will \nreceive but current indications are for stronger southwesterly winds \nand a return to near normal temperatures arriving on Saturday and \nhanging around through at least the middle of next week. As far as \nprecipitation goes, chances look slim as the trough doesn't dig far \nenough south to bring in the necessary moisture. With conditions \nremaining dry and relative humidities dropping into the single \ndigits across much of the region, adding in the increase in winds, \nand we near-critical to critical fire weather conditions will return \nover the weekend.",
